            {
               "text": "### The Birth of a Star\n\nIn this image, the circle marks a region of the Milky Way where new stars are forming inside red clouds (<htcite0>).\n\n#### The Beginning\n\nMaking a star is a long, slow process. It took around 300 million years for the first stars to appear (<htcite1>).\n\nA star is born inside a huge cloud of cold gas and dust, made mostly of hydrogen (H). Stars can form from this cloud in two ways: by gravitational collapse (“matter pulling together”) or by the pressure of gas heated by nearby stars (<htcite2>; <htcite3>; <htcite4>).\n\n#### The Collapse of Clouds\n\nInside the cloud it is freezing cold — around -240 °C (-400 °F). Even so, the nuclei of hydrogen (H) atoms come closer together. They collide, heating the inside of the cloud and pulling in more matter. In time, this process creates the so-called “baby stars” (<htcite5>; <htcite6>; <htcite7>).\n\n#### The Formation of the Star\n\nAs the matter sinks into the center of the protostar (a star that is still being born), the hydrogen (H) atoms begin to fuse and create helium (He) atoms (<htcite8>). The dust that does not become part of the star joins together too, and that is how planets form (<htcite9>).\n\nAll of this is a general picture built from data collected by telescopes. But a star takes thousands of years to form, so nobody has ever watched the whole process from start to finish (<htcite10>).\n\n#### Literature\n\nIn ancient texts you can often find stories where stars and people simply appear, almost out of nowhere (<htcite11>; <htcite12>; <htcite13>). These stories cannot be called wrong, because they belong to literature. Even so, they show that people have wanted to explain the universe for thousands of years (<htcite14>).\n\n**In short:** a star begins as a knot of matter inside a huge, freezing cloud. Gravity squeezes it until the center gets hot enough for hydrogen to fuse, and the leftover dust around it clumps together to form planets.",
